{"hash":"98dabe066bee4a9e","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":83.499,"said":"Look up. From the crown of Baba’s tower to the crown of this temple, Maa Parvati’s, long red threads are strung across the sky. This is the gathbandhan, the knot that ties bride and groom together at a Hindu wedding, tied here between Shiva and Shakti. Devotees offer the threads with their prayers, and men climb the towers to tie them, crown to crown. Why does the Goddess matter so much here? Remember Sati, Shiva’s first wife, who gave up her life at her father’s sacrifice. Shiva wandered the worlds in grief, carrying her body, until Vishnu’s discus divided it, and wherever a part fell became a Shakti Peetha. Tradition holds that her heart fell here. So Deoghar is the Hridaya Peetha, the seat of the heart, and the Mother is worshipped here as Jaya Durga. The Shiva Purana, naming the twelve Jyotirlingas, places Vaidyanath ‘in the cremation ground’, and people here have long linked those words with Sati’s story. A Jyotirlinga and a Shakti Peetha side by side: few places hold both. Now walk with me round the back of Baba’s temple. Its stones have a story of their own.\n\nDid you know? The walled courtyard holds about twenty-two temples: Baba’s, and twenty-one others around it.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1081},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1097,"end":1190}],"words":[[0.098,0.288,0,4],[0.399,0.252,5,7],[1.478,0.228,9,13],[1.719,0.084,14,17],[1.815,0.337,18,23],[2.163,0.144,24,26],[2.32,0.457,27,33],[2.788,0.397,34,39],[3.197,0.108,40,42],[3.317,0.084,43,46],[3.413,0.349,47,52],[3.774,0.108,53,55],[3.894,0.24,56,60],[4.147,0.517,61,67],[4.892,0.168,69,72],[5.072,0.757,73,82],[6.022,0.361,84,88],[6.394,0.252,89,92],[6.659,0.409,93,100],[7.079,0.132,101,104],[7.224,0.373,105,111],[7.608,0.421,112,118],[8.041,0.108,119,122],[8.161,0.457,123,126],[9.519,0.276,128,132],[9.808,0.12,133,135],[9.94,0.084,136,139],[10.036,0.745,140,151],[11.022,0.12,153,156],[11.154,0.